2.1.3. Transpose of a Matrix
52:39
Write a C program to read a square matrix of integers and compute its transpose.

The transpose of a matrix is obtained by flipping it over its main diagonal, which means converting the rows into columns and columns into rows.



Input Format:

The first line contains an integer 
 - the number of rows and columns in the square matrix.
The next 
 lines each contain 
 integers - the elements of the matrix row-wise.


Output Format:

The output displays the transpose with rows on new lines and elements separated by spaces.


Sample Input:

3
1 2 3
4 5 6
7 8 9
Sample Output:

1 4 7
2 5 8
3 6 9

Sample Test Cases


#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{

int a[10][10], b[10][10], n, i, j;

scanf("%d", &n);

for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);
}
}

for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
b[j][i] = a[i][j];
}
}

for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
for (j = 0; j < n; j++) {
printf("%d ", b[i][j]);

}
printf("\n");
	}
	return 0;
}

